<header>
  <h1>Why is the Sky Blue? / 为什么天空是蓝色的</h1>
  <p>Bilingual presentation • English on the left, Simplified Chinese on the right</p>
</header>

<div class="container">
  <div class="column">
    <div class="lang-label">English</div>
    <h1>Why is the Sky Blue?</h1>

    <h2>For a 3-Year-Old Toddler</h2>
    <p>The sky is blue because the sun is playing with the air.</p>
    <p>Imagine the sky is like a big blue blanket. Sunlight is like tiny, invisible paint drops. When those drops go through the air, the air loves the blue color the most and holds onto it. So we see blue up high!</p>
    <p>It's like when you drink blue milk with a straw — the blue stays with you. The sky keeps the blue for us to see.</p>
    <p>The sky is blue, and that's what makes daytime happy.</p>
    <hr>

    <h2>For a High School Teenager</h2>
    <p>The sky looks blue because of a phenomenon called Rayleigh scattering.</p>
    <p>Sunlight is actually white, but it's made of a mix of all the colors of the visible spectrum — red, orange, yellow, green, blue, violet. When this light enters Earth's atmosphere, it collides with tiny air molecules, which are much smaller than the wavelength of visible light.</p>
    <p>The amount of scattering is inversely proportional to the fourth power of the wavelength. Shorter wavelengths scatter more. Blue light has a shorter wavelength ~450 nm compared to red ~650 nm, so it scatters about 10x more than red light.</p>
    <p>Because blue light is scattered in all directions by nitrogen and oxygen molecules, we see blue from every direction when we look up. Red and orange light scatter less, so they travel more or less straight to our eyes.</p>
    <p>This is why at sunrise and sunset the sky can look red: the blue light has been scattered out of the direct path, and we see the longer wavelengths.</p>
    <hr>

    <h2>For a Physics PhD</h2>
    <p>The blue sky is a textbook example of Rayleigh scattering from a dilute, isotropic scatterer with size parameter \(x = 2\pi r / \lambda \ll 1\).</p>
    <p class="formula">
      dσ/dΩ = (8π k⁴ a⁶ / 3) | (m²-1)/(m²+2) |² (1 + cos²θ)
    </p>
    <p>where \(k = 2\pi/\lambda\), \(a\) is the molecular radius, \(m\) the complex refractive index, and \(\theta\) the scattering angle. The \(\lambda^{-4}\) dependence gives the familiar strong wavelength dependence.</p>
    <p>For the Earth's atmosphere, the total scattering coefficient per unit length is</p>
    <p class="formula">β_λ ∝ (P/T) · 1/λ⁴</p>
    <p>with P/T via the ideal gas law. This produces the sky radiance \(I(\lambda) \approx I_0(\lambda) · β_λ · ℓ\) in the single-scattering approximation.</p>
    <p>In practice, the observed sky radiances deviate from ideal Rayleigh because of:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Ozone absorption in the UV-blue and Chappuis band,</li>
      <li>Mie scattering from aerosols, water droplets and haze, which adds a nearly wavelength-independent component and depolarization,</li>
      <li>Multiple scattering in the optical depth τ, which modifies the angular distribution and polarization pattern,</li>
      <li>Refraction and extinction by O₂ and O₃, and the Earth's albedo.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The net spectral radiance is therefore \(I_{sky}(λ,θ) = I_⊙(λ) e^{-τ_R(λ) sec z} · [single Rayleigh + Mie + multiple]\), which for λ ≃ 400-500 nm and overhead sun yields a peak in the blue due to the λ⁻⁴ scattering. Polarization of the skylight reaches a maximum of ~1 at 90° from the sun, as expected from Rayleigh scattering under the Rayleigh–Debye theory.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="column">
    <div class="lang-label">中文</div>
    <h1>为什么天空是蓝色的？</h1>

    <h2>给3岁的小孩</h2>
    <p>天空是蓝色的，因为太阳在和空气玩游戏。</p>
    <p>想象天空像一张大大的蓝色毯子。阳光就像无数看不见的小颜料滴。当这些小滴穿过空气时，空气最喜欢蓝色，就牢牢抓住蓝色。所以我们抬头就能看到蓝色！</p>
    <p>就像你用吸管喝蓝色牛奶——蓝色会留在你身边。天空把蓝色留给我们看。</p>
    <p>天空是蓝色的，这就是白天很开心的理由。</p>
    <hr>

    <h2>给高中生</h2>
    <p>天空看起来是蓝色的，这是一种叫做瑞利散射的现象。</p>
    <p>阳光实际上是白色的，但它是由可见光谱中的各种颜色混合而成的——红、橙、黄、绿、蓝、紫。当这束光进入地球大气层时，它会与空气分子相撞，而这些分子远小于可见光的波长。</p>
    <p>散射量与波长的四次方成反比。波长越短，散射越强。蓝光的波长约 450 nm，比红光的约 650 nm 短，因此散射强度大约是红光的10倍。</p>
    <p>因为蓝光被氮和氧分子向各个方向散射，我们从任何方向看上去都是蓝色的。红光和橙光散射得少，基本沿直线传播到我们的眼睛。</p>
    <p>这就是为什么日出日落时天空会变红：蓝光被散射出了直射路径，我们看到的是波长更长的光。</p>
    <hr>

    <h2>给物理学博士</h2>
    <p>蓝色天空是瑞利散射的经典例子，来自稀薄、各向同性的散射体，尺寸参数为 \(x = 2\pi r / \lambda \ll 1\)。</p>
    <p class="formula">
      dσ/dΩ = (8π k⁴ a⁶ /3) |(m²-1)/(m²+2)|² (1 + cos²θ)
    </p>
    <p>其中 \(k = 2\pi/\lambda\)，\(a\) 为分子半径，\(m\) 为复折射率，\(\theta\) 为散射角。\(\lambda^{-4}\) 的依赖关系带来了熟悉的强波长依赖。</p>
    <p>对于地球大气，每单位长度的总散射系数近似为</p>
    <p class="formula">β_λ ∝ (P/T) · 1/λ⁴</p>
    <p>其中 \(P/T\) 由理想气体定律给出。在单次散射近似下，天空辐射度 \(I(\lambda) \approx I_0(\lambda) · β_λ · ℓ\)。</p>
    <p>实际上，观测到的天空辐射度会偏离理想瑞利情况，因为：</p>
    <ul>
      <li>紫外-蓝光和查普伊斯带的臭氧吸收；</li>
      <li>气溶胶、水滴和雾霾产生的米氏散射，增加了近乎与波长无关的分量并导致去偏振；</li>
      <li>光学深度 τ 下的多重散射，改变了角分布和偏振模式；</li>
      <li>\(O_2\) 和 \(O_3\) 的折射和消光，以及地球反照率。</li>
    </ul>
    <p>因此净光谱辐射度为 \(I_{sky}(λ,θ) = I_⊙(λ) e^{-τ_R(λ) sec z} · [单次瑞利 + 米氏 + 多重]\)，当 λ ≃ 400-500 nm 且太阳位于天顶时，由于 λ⁻⁴ 散射，天空辐射在蓝色处达到峰值。天空光的偏振度在距太阳 90° 处达到最大值 ~1，与瑞利-德拜理论预期一致。</p>
